¶
'''alarum''' (a̤-lar′um or a-lär′um), ''n.'' [A form of ''alarm'', due to a strong rolling of the ''r'': see ''alarm'', ''n.''] Same as ''alarm'', but now used only in sense 4, except poetically.
{{smaller block|A flourish, trumpets! strike ''alarum'', drums!
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. III., iv. 4.}}
The dread ''alarum'' should make the earth quake to its centre.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Old Manse.}}
She had an ''alarum'' to call her up early.
{{right|''Charlotte Brontë'', Jane Eyre, xxi.}}}}

'''alarum''' (a̤-lar′um or a-lär′um), ''v. t.'' Same as ''alarm''.
{{smaller block|<poem>Wither’d murther,
''Alarum’d'' by his sentinel, the wolf.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, ii. 1.}}}}

'''alarum-bell''' (a̤-lar′um-bel), ''n.'' Same as ''alarm-bell''.
{{smaller block|No citizen can lie down secure that he shall not be roused by the ''alarum-bell'', to repel or avenge an injury.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Dante.}}}}

'''alary''' (ā′la̤-ri), ''a.'' [< L. ''alarius'': see ''alar''.] '''1.''' Relating to wings or wing-like parts; being wing-like. {{smaller|Specifically applied, in ''entom.'', to certain muscles passing in pairs from the walls of the pericardial chamber of some insects to the abdominal parietes. See ''alæ cordis'', under ''ala''.}}
{{smaller block|The ''alary'' system of insects.
{{right|''Wollaston'', Variation of Species, p. 45.}}
The ''alary'' muscles, which in most insects are fan-shaped, and lie in pairs, opposite one another, on each side of the heart, either unite in the middle line, or are inserted into a sort of fascia, on the sternal aspect of the heart, to which organ they are not directly attached.
{{right|''Huxley'', Anat. Invert., p. 373.}}}}

